Windows for everyday use: the pointer, the button and the keyboard go to the same window, the
one a person sees under the pointer (1); a window is told it is maximized, not fullscreen,
unless it asks (2); a second window keeps its own size and is not lost under the first, and
an application can ask for a window to come forward (3).

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86, on top of gst-wayland-display-render-on-commit.patch,
gst-wayland-display-cursor-messages.patch, gst-wayland-display-windows.patch and
gst-wayland-display-dialogs.patch in this directory, in that order. The lines of context are
from that project and from those patches.

FOR TRIALS AND EVERYDAY USE ONLY. It is not in the build the M1 gate and verdict measure, and
must not be: scripts/build-compositor.sh applies it only with ISTESSO_COMPOSITOR_TRIAL=1
(docs/m3-compositor-windows.md). Like the others it is kept small and in a shape that can be
proposed to that project (docs/adr/0009-host-compositor.md). There is no property.

1. Where the pointer, the button and the keyboard go

The fault (seen with Blender's Preferences, which is a second window without a parent): a
window is put into the picture at the commit after its first configure, whether that commit
brings a picture or not. Blender's second window answers its configure and commits before it
has drawn; the pointer, told again where it is at that moment, finds nothing of the new window
under it and goes into the window below. It stayed there until it moved. A click without a
move then went to the window below, while the keyboard went to the window on top (the keyboard
was given to the top window under the pointer, the button to the window the pointer was in).

What it changes:

- The pointer is told again where it is whenever a window that is shown commits (a new
  picture, a new size, a first picture), when a window comes or goes, and on a click after the
  window clicked has been raised. Nothing is sent when the pointer is in the right window
  already, so a commit costs one look at what is under the pointer. A pointer that has never
  been in a window stays out of a lone window until it moves or clicks, as before.
- On a click the window under the pointer comes up (with its dialogs, as before), the pointer
  is put into what is on top at its place now, and the keyboard goes to that same window: the
  button and the keys never go to two windows. A click with no move before it reaches a lone
  window too (before, it went nowhere). A frame is made when the click changed the order.
- Whenever the keyboard goes to another window (a click, a window that comes or goes), every
  window is told whether it is the active one (the `xdg_toplevel` state "activated"; only a
  change is sent). Before, every window was told it was active when it came and never told
  otherwise. Blender goes by this state: in the window it still took for inactive, a Shift
  held down did not reach the next key (Shift+A came as A), seen in the host's self-test.

Tests (`cargo test -p wayland-display-core`, file tests/test_trial_windows.rs, with a real
Wayland client): a second window that commits before its first picture has the pointer from
that picture on, and a click without a move gives it the button and the keyboard, and makes
it the active window; a click without any move reaches a lone window with the keyboard; a
click beside a dialog gives the button, the keyboard and the active state to its parent.

2. Maximized, not fullscreen

The fault: the compositor told every window without a parent that it was fullscreen, as the
project does upstream. Firefox hides its toolbar then (it slides in only when the pointer
touches the top edge), and other applications drop their own bars.

What it changes:

- A window that is made as large as the output is told it is maximized, at the size of the
  output (kept to its largest size, if it has one). It is told fullscreen only when it asks
  (`xdg_toplevel.set_fullscreen`: a video player, a game, a presentation); it is put at the
  corner in front then. When it asks no longer, a window without a parent is maximized again;
  a dialog keeps a size of its own.
- A window that asks to be maximized is.
- When the output changes its size, a window that fills it (maximized, or fullscreen because
  it asked) is told the new size; any other window keeps its size and is told how much room
  there is. Before, every window was told fullscreen with no size (an empty intersection with
  "no largest size").

Tests: the first window is told maximized at the size of the output and not fullscreen; one
that asks for fullscreen has it and is maximized again when it leaves it; a maximized window
is told the new size of the output.

3. A second window that is not lost (a stopgap until windows can be switched from outside)

The fault: a second window without a parent (Blender's Preferences, a second Firefox window)
was told to fill the output like the first and lay exactly over it. A click on the first
window, if it ever came up, hid the second for good: only the top window can be clicked.

What it changes:

- A window without a parent that comes while another window fills the output is not made as
  large as the output. It is told how much room there is (the configure's bounds), keeps the
  size it wants, and lies over the middle of the output. So does any window without a parent
  that does not fill the output (one with a largest size, say). It keeps its size when it
  leaves fullscreen or asks to be maximized no longer. (A window that comes while only such
  smaller windows are shown, after a splash screen say, still fills the output.)
- A window that was shown before it had a picture (it committed without one first, as
  Blender does with its Preferences) is placed again by the size of its first picture.
  Before, it was placed as a window of no size: over the middle meant its corner lay in the
  middle, and the rest of it beyond the output. (This holds for dialogs too.)
- When a window that fills the output is raised by a click, the smaller windows without a
  parent of the same client are put above it again, in the order they had, with their dialogs;
  its own dialogs come above all of them. Windows of other clients stay where they are. The
  click itself goes as in part 1: the button and the keyboard go to what is on top under the
  pointer after this, so a click where a smaller window comes back goes to that window.
- `xdg_activation_v1` is offered: an application can ask for one of its windows to come
  forward (raised with its dialogs, with the keyboard). A token counts for 10 seconds and once.
  It is how a second start of an application shows the window of the first, and how the
  first window can be brought back over a smaller one. A window that is not shown yet is not
  remembered: it comes in front with the keyboard when it is shown anyway.

Tests: a second window without a parent keeps its size, lies over the middle, has the pointer
that stood there and the keyboard, and keeps its size when it leaves fullscreen; one shown
before its picture lies over the middle by the size of its picture; the main window brought
forward by activation lies over the smaller one and is the active window, and a click on it
brings the smaller window back on top while the main window has the button and the keyboard,
and the smaller window takes a click where it lies. Two tests of gst-wayland-display-dialogs.patch
are changed to this rule (a second window without a parent: told the room, not the size of
the output; over the middle, not at the corner).

Known limits:

- Nothing outside the compositor can pick a window yet; a window under another that fills the
  output is reached only when the one on top goes, or by its application.
- "The same client" is one Wayland connection. An application that opens its windows on two
  connections is two clients here.
- A window that leaves fullscreen or maximized stays where it was put then (the corner), and a
  window is not placed again when it changes its size after its first picture.
- `xdg_toplevel.unset_maximized` of the first window is answered with the same size: it stays
  maximized.

